## Building a Contact Manager

Contacts need two capabilities: instant lookup by name, and ordered display. That means two collections working together.

### Data Model

```xiom
let contacts: Map[Str,Str] = Map[Str,Str].new();
```

Name → phone number. Simple. But we also need to list names alphabetically, so we maintain a Vec:

```xiom
let name_list: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();
```

### Adding a Contact

```xiom
fn add_contact(contacts: Map[Str,Str], name_list: Vec[Str], name: Str, phone: Str) -> Bool {
  if contacts.contains(name) { return false; }
  contacts.insert(name, phone);
  name_list.push(name);
  return true;
}
```

### Looking Up a Contact

```xiom
fn find_contact(contacts: Map[Str,Str], name: Str) {
  match contacts.get(name) {
    Some(phone) -> io.println(name + ": " + phone);
    None -> io.println("Contact not found");
  }
}
```

### Listing All Contacts

```xiom
fn list_contacts(contacts: Map[Str,Str], name_list: Vec[Str]) {
  io.println("Contacts:");
  for name in name_list {
    match contacts.get(name) {
      Some(phone) -> io.println("  " + name + ": " + phone);
      None -> {}
    }
  }
}
```

### Full Program

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let contacts: Map[Str,Str] = Map[Str,Str].new();
  let name_list: Vec[Str] = Vec[Str].new();

  contacts.insert("Alice", "555-0100");
  name_list.push("Alice");

  contacts.insert("Bob", "555-0200");
  name_list.push("Bob");

  find_contact(contacts, "Alice");
  list_contacts(contacts, name_list);

  io.println("Total contacts: " + string.int_to_str(name_list.len()));
  return 0;
}
```

